# Python bytecode. The repository had no rule for this at all, which is why
# docker/images/__pycache__/ and scripts/__pycache__/ each carry a committed .pyc. Those two
# stay tracked -- an ignore does not untrack anything -- but nothing new joins them.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# NOTHING FOR //third_party/crate_mirror HERE, deliberately.
#
# There used to be `!third_party/crate_mirror/` + `/**`, on the theory that repository-wide
# ignores would otherwise catch some archive names. Measured against all 886: zero match any
# pattern in this file, because every one ends in `.crate` and nothing here matches that.
# The un-ignores rescued nothing, and `/**` was broad enough to re-admit __pycache__ -- which
# is how a .pyc from scripts/check-rust-vendor-tracked.py's `import sync` reached `git add`.
#
# If a future pattern ever does catch an archive, re-add a narrow negation for THAT pattern
# rather than a directory wildcard. The wildcard is what makes an un-ignore dangerous: it
# re-admits everything the rest of this file is trying to exclude.
.local-fixture-certs/
.local-secrets/
